[%out 32553] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 2769] **Situation:** Red's
rook on d9 attacks my cannon on d7, and nothing protects it. **Options for that
cannon:** - **Along the d-file:** every square is still on the rook's line, so
it would just be taken. - **Up to d8:** illegal. It would sit between Red's
cannon on a8 and my king on f8, which puts my own king in check. - **Along rank
7:** the only real choice. a7 is bad, because Red's cannon could then capture
my pawn on a6 by jumping over my cannon. That leaves c7 and b7, and c7 is more
flexible. **Other considerations:** - My cannon on e6 pins Red's horse on e5.
If the horse moved, my cannon would give check through e3. So the e6 cannon
stays, and I should not play e6xe3, which would free the horse. - Red threatens
rook d9 to d8, a double check with the rook and the a8 cannon. I can answer
that with king f8 to f7. - If Red instead plays rook to d7, attacking both my
c7 cannon and my elephant on e7, I will save the cannon and give up the
elephant. } 45. 炮九进一 将６平５ { [%emt 0:06:59] [%think 39367] [%out 39730]
